---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Vote Yes on Prop 1 | Tiny Village Spirit"
  description="Proposition 1, the Veterans and Affordable Housing Bond Act of 2026, funds 40,000+ affordable homes across California — without raising taxes. Tiny Village Spirit urges a Yes vote."
  ogTitle="Vote Yes on Prop 1 | Tiny Village Spirit"
  ogUrl="https://tinyvillagespirit.org/prop-1"
  ogDescription="Prop 1 fast-tracks 40,000 affordable homes for veterans, seniors, and working families. No tax increase. Vote YES this November."
  css={['/styling/prop-1.css']}
>
<main>

    <!-- ── Hero ── -->
    <section class="p1-hero">
        <div class="p1-hero-inner reveal">
            <div class="p1-eyebrow">November 2026 Ballot · California</div>
            <h1>Vote Yes on<br>Proposition 1.</h1>
            <p>
                The Veterans and Affordable Housing Bond Act of 2026 funds thousands of affordable homes
                across California — for veterans, seniors, working families, and our unhoused neighbors —
                without raising taxes.
            </p>
            <a href="#faq" class="p1-hero-cta">Learn more ↓</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Stats ── -->
    <section class="p1-stats">
        <div class="p1-stats-inner">
            <div class="reveal">
                <div class="p1-stat-num">40,000+</div>
                <div class="p1-stat-label">Affordable homes created</div>
            </div>
            <div class="reveal">
                <div class="p1-stat-num">20,000</div>
                <div class="p1-stat-label">Households reach homeownership</div>
            </div>
            <div class="reveal">
                <div class="p1-stat-num">$1.25B</div>
                <div class="p1-stat-label">Dedicated to veterans' housing</div>
            </div>
            <div class="reveal">
                <div class="p1-stat-num">$0</div>
                <div class="p1-stat-label">New taxes on Californians</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── What is it ── -->
    <section class="p1-what">
        <div class="reveal">
            <div class="p1-section-label">What is Proposition 1?</div>
            <h2>More homes. No new taxes. Real accountability.</h2>
            <p>
                Proposition 1 authorizes state bond funding to build and preserve affordable homes across
                California — with strict accountability and transparency. The bond is repaid over time
                through the state's existing budget, the same way California has financed schools, roads,
                and other public investments for decades.
            </p>
            <p>
                Prop 1 also unlocks federal matching funds, bringing tax dollars we already send to
                Washington back home to California. The 2018 housing bond worked — and the funding has
                been fully exhausted. More than 40,000 homes are shovel-ready right now, waiting only for
                funding to break ground. Prop 1 is the next step.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── Who it serves ── -->
    <section class="p1-who">
        <div class="p1-who-inner">
            <h2 class="reveal">Prop 1 builds homes for those hit hardest<br>by California's housing crisis.</h2>
            <div class="p1-who-grid">
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Veterans and their families</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Seniors on fixed incomes</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Teachers and firefighters</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Nurses and essential workers</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">People with disabilities</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Farmworkers</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">College students</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">First-time homebuyers</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Foster youth</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Survivors of domestic violence</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Low- and middle-income families</div>
                <div class="p1-who-item reveal">Unhoused Californians</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── FAQ ── -->
    <section class="p1-faq" id="faq">
        <h2 class="reveal">Frequently asked questions</h2>

        <div class="p1-faq-item reveal">
            <p class="p1-faq-q">Will this raise my taxes?</p>
            <p class="p1-faq-a">
                No. Proposition 1 does not raise taxes. The bond is repaid over time through the state's
                existing budget — the same way California has financed schools, roads, and public
                infrastructure for decades. Prop 1 also unlocks federal matching funds, returning tax
                dollars we send to Washington back home to California.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p1-faq-item reveal">
            <p class="p1-faq-q">How many homes will Prop 1 create?</p>
            <p class="p1-faq-a">
                Prop 1 is expected to create and preserve more than 40,000 new affordable homes and help
                20,000 households achieve homeownership. Student housing funding is projected to benefit
                40,000 CSU and UC students over the 55-year life of newly built housing.
                (Source: California Housing Partnership)
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p1-faq-item reveal">
            <p class="p1-faq-q">How does this serve veterans?</p>
            <p class="p1-faq-a">
                California has one of the largest populations of homeless veterans in the nation. Prop 1
                dedicates $1.25 billion solely to veterans' housing and homeownership through the CalVet
                Home Loan Program, which has already helped nearly one million veterans and their families.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p1-faq-item reveal">
            <p class="p1-faq-q">How quickly will homes get built?</p>
            <p class="p1-faq-a">
                More than 40,000 homes are already shovel-ready — they're stalled only for lack of
                funding. Prop 1 unlocks that funding so construction can begin quickly. Families will see
                results in years, not decades.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p1-faq-item reveal">
            <p class="p1-faq-q">How will we know the money is spent correctly?</p>
            <p class="p1-faq-a">
                Prop 1 includes strict accountability measures. Every dollar comes with public reporting
                and clear requirements. Voters can see exactly how many homes get built, where, and at
                what cost.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p1-faq-item reveal">
            <p class="p1-faq-q">Didn't California already pass a housing bond in 2018?</p>
            <p class="p1-faq-a">
                Yes — and it worked. The 2018 bond funded thousands of affordable homes for veterans,
                seniors, and working families. The problem is simple: the need is so great that the
                funding has been fully exhausted. Prop 1 is the next step in an ongoing commitment to
                ensure every Californian has a safe, affordable place to live.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p1-faq-item reveal">
            <p class="p1-faq-q">How do I vote yes on Prop 1?</p>
            <p class="p1-faq-a">
                Vote YES on Proposition 1 on your November 2026 ballot. All active registered voters will
                receive a mail-in ballot. Early voting, in-person voting, and ballot drop-off information
                is available through your county elections office.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ── CTA ── -->
    <section class="p1-cta">
        <div class="p1-cta-inner reveal">
            <h2>Vote YES on<br>Prop 1 this November.</h2>
            <p>
                Tiny Village Spirit has seen firsthand what happens when communities invest in real
                housing solutions. Prop 1 is one of the most direct things California can do right now
                to address the housing crisis at scale.
            </p>
            <a href="https://www.sos.ca.gov/elections/voter-registration" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="p1-cta-btn">
                Check your registration →
            </a>
            <p class="p1-disclaimer">
                Ad Paid for by Yes on Prop 1 Californians for Homes, Jobs and Affordability 2026,
                Sponsored by Affordable Housing Organizations. Funding details at fppc.ca.gov.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

</main>
</BaseLayout>

<script is:inline>
const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach((e) => {
        if (e.isIntersecting) { e.target.classList.add('in-view'); io.unobserve(e.target); }
    });
}, { threshold: 0.1 });
document.querySelectorAll('.reveal').forEach((el) => io.observe(el));
</script>
